//! tr kodlu 2 dil=en
// 29 — Optimizers: ways of updating the weights.
// What it teaches: AdamW and SGD, the beta1/beta2 and momentum settings, limiting an exploding gradient with
// clip_grad, taking the state of an optimizer and putting it back with load_state.
// Builds on: the training loop (28), automatic differentiation (26).
// Run (from the project root): tr examples/en/29_optimizers.tr

// SGD (stochastic gradient descent) is the plainest optimizer: w = w - lr · gradient.
// momentum keeps the direction of earlier steps going.
A030 w = T100([1.0], T900: A040)
A030 sgd = T401([w], T910: 0.1, T914: 0.9)
T300(G400(w * w)) // d(w²)/dw = 2w = 2
sgd.Y500()
G100("w after one SGD step =", G405(w.Y404[0], 4))

// AdamW adapts a separate step size for every weight. beta1 is the exponential decay rate of the gradient
// average, beta2 of the average of the squared gradients (in the range [0, 1)).
A030 v = T100([1.0], T900: A040)
A030 adamw = T400([v], T910: 0.1, T912: 0.8, T913: 0.99)
T300(G400(v * v))
adamw.Y500()
G100("v after one AdamW step =", G405(v.Y404[0], 4))
G100(G201(sgd) == A214, G201(adamw) == A214)

// clip_grad shrinks the gradients by the same ratio if the total gradient norm exceeds max;
// it gives the norm before clipping. It keeps training stable on steps with large gradients.
A030 u = T100([3.0, 4.0], T900: A040)
T300(G400(u * u)) // gradient [6, 8], norm 10
G100("norm:", T402([u], T904: 1.0))
G100("clipped gradient:", u.Y403.Y404)

// state gives the inner state (the moments) of the optimizer as a dict; load_state puts it back into
// another optimizer built with the same settings (checkpoints work like this).
A030 fresh = T400([v], T910: 0.1, T912: 0.8, T913: 0.99)
G100("state loaded:", fresh.Y503(adamw.Y502))

// --- Try it yourself ---
// 1. Set the momentum of SGD to 0: does w come out different on the same step?
// 2. Make max 100 in clip_grad: does the gradient change?
